/actions/start-project-with-autopilot.ts:111-143`, `src/actions/start-project-with-autopilot.ts:169-177`) — `targetScale`, `marketSegment`, `geography`, `startupStage`, and `additionalContext` are saved to the project row, but the `pdf_engine_runs.brief_text` insert uses only `subject.trim()`. If the founder supplies hard numeric constraints through the wizard UI rather than prose, the production chain never sees them, so the PDF can ignore load-bearing intake data.\n\n5. **Autopilot start can report success even when no PDF job was queued** — (`src/actions/start-project-with-autopilot.ts:179-185`, `src/actions/start-project-with-autopilot.ts:186-192`) — On `pdf_engine_runs` insert error, the action returns `{ ok: true, projectId }`; on thrown insert error, it logs and falls through to `{ ok: true, projectId }`. The user lands on a workspace, but no worker job may exist, so the page can sit forever with no PDF generation.\n\n6. **No stale-`running` recovery / lease timeout in worker** — (`scripts/pdf-engine-worker.mjs:78-129`, `scripts/pdf-engine-worker.mjs:302-330`) — The worker only claims `status='pending'` rows and never reclaims rows stuck in `running` after a worker crash, Mac sleep, process kill, or hung child. There is no heartbeat, lease expiry, or retry transition back to `pending`, so a production run can silently wedge permanently.\n\n7. **No child-process timeout around the 60–90 min chain** — (`scripts/pdf-engine-worker.mjs:133-158`, `scripts/pdf-engine-worker.mjs:187-195`) — `runChild()` waits only for `exit`/`error`; there is no timeout or kill path. If the chain, `npx`, a model call, Python Engine C, or a subprocess hangs, the single worker remains blocked forever and stops processing all future jobs.\n\n8. **Worker state snapshot expects `gatesPassed`, but the chain never writes it** — (`scripts/pdf-engine-worker.mjs:249-259`; `scripts/serial-design-chain-v2.tsx:1855-1884`) — The worker stores `state_snapshot_json.gatesPassed = parsed.gatesPassed ?? null`, but final state contains `acceptanceStatus` only, not `gatesPassed`. Any UI/diagnostic consuming the snapshot sees null even when Phase 2 passed or failed.\n\n9. **Final gate verdicts are discarded from state** — (`scripts/serial-design-chain-v2.tsx:1510-1544`, `scripts/serial-design-chain-v2.tsx:1855-1884`) — Phase 2 computes arithmetic/grammar gate results every iteration, but final state writes `grammarVerdicts: null` and does not persist the final `arith`, `grammar`, `failed`, or `allPassed` objects. Renderer/UI sections that could show engineering-check details have no data except coarse `acceptanceStatus` and derived design decisions.\n\n10. **Engine B mutates `partVerifications` after the chain’s summary is already frozen** — (`scripts/serial-design-chain-v2.tsx:1868-1878`; `scripts/estimate-missing-prices.tsx:main` writeback block) — The chain writes `partVerificationSummary` before spawning Engine B. Engine B then updates/synthesises `state.partVerifications` with price estimates and component classes but does not recompute `state.partVerificationSummary`. Any renderer block reading the summary can show stale totals, stale unverified/uncertain counts, and stale recommendation counts.\n\n11. **Engine B / Engine C index parts only by `word_id`, not module/sub-module path** — (`scripts/estimate-missing-prices.tsx:main`, `verifByWordId.set(v.word_id, v)`; `scripts/enrich-state-with-reference-anchor.tsx:214-218`, `scripts/enrich-state-with-reference-anchor.tsx:231-252`) — Both enrichers key maps and request IDs by bare `w.id`. If different modules reuse common word IDs such as `housing`, `sensor`, `controller`, or `mounting_bracket`, later rows collide and earlier rows are overwritten/skipped. Result: prices/reference flags can attach to the wrong BoM line or disappear.\n\n12. **Engine C depends on OpenAI embeddings but the worker does not load an OpenAI secret file** — (`scripts/enrich-state-with-reference-anchor.tsx:10-22`, `scripts/enrich-state-with-reference-anchor.tsx:148-159`; `scripts/pdf-engine-worker.mjs:43-50`) — Engine C’s Python helper uses OpenAI embeddings, but the worker env bootstrap loads `.env.local`, distributor APIs, Tavily, and OpenRouter only. If `OPENAI_API_KEY` is not in `.env.local`, Engine C fails and the chain catches it fail-soft, leaving no `engine_c_summary` / reference flags in the PDF.\n\n13. **Engine C product-class hint table is narrower than the chain/K10 alias universe** — (`scripts/enrich-state-with-reference-anchor.tsx:54-89`; compare `scripts/serial-design-chain-v2.tsx:1620-1645`) — Engine C lacks aliases present elsewhere, e.g. `mini_split_heatpump`, `battery_energy_storage`, `residential_ess`, `commercial_heatpump`, `vfd`, `drone`, `agv`, `amr`, `auv`, `traction_battery_pack`. Those classes fall back to full-corpus search or no useful class bias, degrading reference-price anchoring.\n\n14. **Deployment envelope lookup has no alias bridge** — (`scripts/serial-design-chain-v2.tsx:1910-1940`) — The chain calls `defaultEnvelopeForClass(productClass)` directly using `moduleDecomposition.product_class` / `parsedBrief.product_class`, unlike K10 which has an explicit alias map. If the classifier emits `mini_split_heatpump`, `heat_pump`, `energy_storage`, `vfd`, etc. but `deployment-envelopes.ts` uses canonical/kebab slugs, `state.deploymentEnvelope` becomes null and the renderer has no shipping/install envelope to show.\n\n15. **Renderer report title ignores the actual chain brief subject** — (`scripts/render-minimal-pdf.tsx:main`, `rawSubject = state.brief?.product_definition?.subject || ...`; `scripts/serial-design-chain-v2.tsx:1849-1865`) — The renderer expects `state.brief.product_definition.subject`, but the chain writes `state.brief = briefBlock` with `original_text`, `parsed_original`, `revised_text`, etc. No `product_definition.subject` exists there, so the PDF title falls back to humanised `product_class` instead of the founder’s actual subject.\n\n16. **Reference-anchor subprocess has no explicit prerequisite check for the corpus DB / Python dependencies** — (`scripts/enrich-state-with-reference-anchor.tsx:148-159`, `scripts/enrich-state-with-reference-anchor.tsx:258-263`) — Engine C directly spawns `python3 scripts/rag/reference_lookup.py --batch`; if Python deps, the corpus DB, or embedding env are missing, the subprocess exits non-zero and the chain logs “continuing without”. The PDF silently loses all reference anchoring instead of surfacing “reference corpus unavailable” in state.\n\n17. **Production worker uploads only the PDF; full `state.json` and `actions.jsonl` are not persisted anywhere durable** — (`scripts/pdf-engine-worker.mjs:224-279`) — The worker uploads `chain-v2.pdf` and writes only a tiny `state_snapshot_json`. The detailed state, gate history, part-verification evidence, Engine B/C annotations, and action log remain on the Mac’s local `~/.pdf-engine-worker/runs/<job>` directory. If that machine is cleaned or replaced, the web app cannot inspect or reproduce why a PDF said what it said.\n\n18. **`state.projectId` is synthetic, not the Supabase project/run id** — (`scripts/serial-design-chain-v2.tsx:1856`; `scripts/pdf-engine-worker.mjs:171-180`) — The chain sets `projectId: 'chain-v2-' + Date.now()` because it only receives a brief path and out-dir. The real `cad_lab_projects.id` / `pdf_engine_runs.id` are not passed into state. Any renderer/footer/diagnostic expecting state to identify the real project gets an orphan synthetic id, weakening traceability.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Evaluating engine class hints**\n\nI’m thinking about how engine C class hints are missing some aliases, like mini_split_heatpump and others. This could cause issues with the full corpus if there’s no hint available. I should remember to cite the lines that map it out. The deployment envelope uses productClass directly, which means there’s no fallback for aliases. I need to be careful about this!**Evaluating API functionality**\n\nI’m noticing that the API already does some pre-composition, which could fill a gap. But it seems to claim doesn’t select parent info, so it wouldn’t compose without that. This part feels a bit speculative, so I want to avoid making assumptions. \n\nI’m also seeing that with failJob updates by ID, there's no worker guard or status, which might risk overwriting what’s ready. Plus, the Worker claim picks the oldest pending, but if processes die, it lacks a visibility timeout or reclaim, which might be problematic.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Evaluating engine class hints**\n\nI’m thinking about how engine C class hints are missing some aliases, like mini_split_heatpump and others. This could cause issues with the full corpus if there’s no hint available. I should remember to cite the lines that map it out. The deployment envelope uses productClass directly, which means there’s no fallback for aliases. I need to be careful about this!**Evaluating API functionality**\n\nI’m noticing that the API already does some pre-composition, which could fill a gap. But it seems to claim doesn’t select parent info, so it wouldn’t compose without that. This part feels a bit speculative, so I want to avoid making assumptions. \n\nI’m also seeing that with failJob updates by ID, there's no worker guard or status, which might risk overwriting what’s ready. Plus, the Worker claim picks the oldest pending, but if processes die, it lacks a visibility timeout or reclaim, which might be problematic.","format":"openai-responses-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"gAAAAABqDKMrALQD72vGtOSoOiicwoKmTN6APoQf5aFBT3FD4-nHifo_viuB_HGaQT1VCkq0gZREiG2bQI3xta3YZHQE5Grs-4MMohghiGLQjqrvCb-ctGGOHnOmpNXY0GpEsxc-MlJu-apqMfKZqB03ORfreAX0xy7Xqb0ODaab7oAJZr8fz82T13jQZ6FzYudWiCw5iTnp7q1Uv_rYx7hoVqFlEuVYE6M7HiXXFH3NCKcIxE6O-WrfCEGSVdC2Nv0IXjRJHiP2Irk0vZtkaEUdr89szr9eDigi6pmtlcKT2vhm-weGenddeqkbEUhD7NUXF2FyagvSRUCV7ZiYUyP10zNZsQelwdFkVMMtB9p6WDJnD50CDDxqF23xHTeSo